n-avoiding permutations and Chebyshev polynomials, Proceeding's 15th Conference on Formal Power Series and Algebraic Combinatorics 2003, June 23--27, 2003 (14pp.).\n4. T. Mansour, Counting occurrences of 132 in an even permutation, Proceeding's 15th Conference on Formal Power Series and Algebraic Combinatorics 2003, June 23--27, 2003 (12pp.).\n3. A. Burstein and T. Mansour, Words restricted by patterns with at most 2 distinct letters, Proceeding's 14th Conference on Formal Power Series and Algebraic Combinatorics, Melbourne, 2002 (11pp.).\n2. T. Mansour and A. Vainshtein, Counting occurrences of 132 in a permutation, Proceeding's 14th Conference on Formal Power Series and Algebraic Combinatorics, Melbourne, 2002 (9pp.).\n1. T. Mansour, Avoiding and containing certain patterns, Proceeding's 12th Conference on Formal Power Series and Algebraic Combinatorics (Moscow 2000), Springer-Verlag, Berlin, 2000, 704--708.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAll 3-transitive groups satisfy the strict-Erdős–Ko–Rado property (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0195669824001422)\nciteturn1search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 months ago; We now describe the Module Method: a sufficient condition for a 2-transitive group to satisfy the strict-EKR property. ... In the same paper, they use this result to prove strict-EKR property of some 2-transitive groups.\n\nTheorem 2.1\n\nLet G\\le \\mathrm{Sym}\\left(n\\right) be a 2-transitive permutation group and let M\\left(G\\right) be its derangement matrix. If \\mathrm{rank}\\left(M\\left(G\\right)\\right)=(n-1)(n-2), then G satisfies the strict-EKR property.\n\nGiven a group G, by ℂ\\left[G\\right], we denote its group algebra. Let \\Omega be a G-set. Let ℂ\\left[\\Omega \\right] be the \\left|\\Omega \\right|-dimensional ℂ-vector space generated by \\{{{\\rm Y}}_{\\omega }:\\phantom{\\rule{1em}{0ex}}\\omega \\in \\Omega \\} as a basis. The action of G on \\Omega leads to a realization of ℂ\\left[\\Omega \\right] as a G-module. Given S\\subseteq \\Omega , we use {{\\rm Y}}_{S} to denote {\\sum }_{\\omega \\in S}{{\\rm Y}}_{\\omega }. The group G acts on both {\\left[n\\right]}^{\\left(2\\right)} and on \\mathfrak{Der}\\left(G\\right) (via conjugation). The matrix M\\left(G\\right) can be considered as a matrix representation of some \\mathfrak{M}\\in {\\mathrm{Hom}}_{ℂ\\left[G\\right]}\\left(ℂ\\left[{\\left[n\\right]}^{\\left(2\\right)}\\right],\\phantom{\\rule{1em}{0ex}}ℂ\\left[\\mathfrak{Der}\\left(G\\right)\\right]\\right). If U is an irreducible submodule of ℂ\\left[{\\left[n\\right]}^{\\left(2\\right)}\\right], then by Schur’s lemma, either \\mathfrak{M}\\left(U\\right)\\cong U or \\mathfrak{M}\\left(U\\right)\\cong \\left\\{0\\right\\}. So, characterizing irreducible submodules which are “annihilated” by M\\left(G\\right) will give us the rank of M\\left(G\\right). We prove Theorem 1.1 using the Module Method.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHilton–Milner characterization conjecture for intersecting sets in 3-transitive groups | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/360832/hilton-milner-characterization-conjecture-for-intersecting-s)\nciteturn1search4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last month; # Hilton–Milner characterization conjecture for intersecting sets in 3-transitive groups ... Let $G$G be a $3$3-transitive permutation group of degree $n$n. ... Venkata Raghu Tej Pantangi, “All 3-transitive groups satisfy the strict-EKR property”, arXiv:2311.13055 (2023).\n\n# Hilton–Milner characterization conjecture for intersecting sets in 3-transitive groups\n\nAbout 3 years old · traced to 2023\n\nLet $G$G be a $3$3-transitive permutation group of degree $n$n. Two elements of $G$G are intersecting when they agree on at least one point, and an intersecting set is a subset $S\\subseteq G$S⊆G whose elements are pairwise intersecting. A canonical intersecting set is a coset of a point stabilizer.\n\nHilton–Milner characterization conjecture. There exists an absolute constant $c_{0}<1$c 0​<1 such that, if $S$S is an intersecting set in $G$G satisfying\n\n$$|S|\\geq c_{0}|G|/n,$$∣S∣≥c 0​∣G∣/n,\n\nthen $S$S is a subset of a canonical intersecting set in $G$G.\n\nThis proposes a uniform stability-type description for sufficiently large intersecting sets, extending Hilton–Milner phenomena known for several particular permutation groups. The source does not report a resolution, so the conjecture remains open.\n\nReferences\n\n## Primary source\n\nVenkata Raghu Tej Pantangi, “All 3-transitive groups satisfy the strict-EKR property”, arXiv:2311.13055 (2023).\n\nextremal-combinatoricspermutation-groupsstability\n\n0 0\n\nPosted 1 month ago\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Never refreshed\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\n## Solutions 0\n\n[Button: Post a solution]\n\n[Button: Solve with an agent]\n\nNo solutions have been posted yet.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSUBMITTED\n\n1. Alexander Burstein, Isaiah Lankham, (https://www.alexanderburstein.com/cv07.pdf)\nciteturn1search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 19.0 years ago; Crawled: 6 months ago;    Restricted patience sorting and barred pattern avoidance. ...    Packing patterns into words. ... 4. ... Alexander Burstein, Sergi Elizalde, Toufik Mansour,\nSUBMITTED\n\n1. Alexander Burstein, Isaiah Lankham,  \n   Restricted patience sorting and barred pattern avoidance.\n\nIN PREPARATION\n\n1. Alexander Burstein,  \n   Pattern statistics in old and new Dumont permutations.\n\n2. Alexander Burstein, Niklas Eriksen,  \n   Combinatorial properties of permutation tableaux.\n\n3. Alexander Burstein, Peter Hästö,  \n   Packing patterns into words.\n\nCONFERENCE TALKS\n\n1. Alexander Burstein, Niklas Eriksen,  \n   Combinatorial properties of permutation tableaux,  \n   Spec